  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The coupled SE(3) encoding of navigation stances with arm trajectories — replacing disjoint occupancy-map navigation — plus stance-conditioned IRM integration within the Mimic retargeting loop for bipedal humanoids is a genuine combination not found in prior work; searches confirm no concurrent paper does this for loco-manipulation data generation. Individual components (SE(3) object-centric encoding, IRM) are known, but bipedal IRM for demo-multiplication is a real gap."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The 20-day Gantt with 2 researchers remains aggressive for three technically non-trivial contributions; the τ sensitivity numbers are labeled 'preliminary analysis estimates' rather than actual experimental results, meaning the compatibility filtering yield (and thus the 100-1,000× expansion claim) rests on unvalidated projections at submission time; sim-only evaluation is acknowledged but still the primary reviewer acceptance risk."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "After nine rounds, the paper is sharply articulated: the PR #3259 gap is precisely characterized, the Contribution 1 crystallization sentence is quotable, the comparison table makes the depth-vs.-breadth tradeoff scannable, and both Gantt charts are present with milestone dependencies explicit."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Addresses the #1 cited bottleneck (data scarcity) in 2026 humanoid VLA training; procedurally building on Isaac Lab's existing infrastructure increases adoption potential; 100-1,000× demo expansion from ≤20 seeds, if experimentally validated, is high-impact for bipedal manipulation research."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "TERMINOLOGICAL INCONSISTENCY — 'preliminary analysis' vs. 'validated operating point': The paper states the 100-1,000× expansion range is 'tied to this validated operating point' (the 10th-percentile τ), but the supporting sensitivity numbers (35-40% yield drop at 25th percentile; 5-8pp success-rate variance at 5th percentile) are explicitly described as 'preliminary analysis estimates.' 'Validated' and 'preliminary analysis' cannot coexist. For a CoRL submission with actual experimental results, the ablation will either be run (making 'validated' accurate) or not (requiring the claim to be weakened to 'planned operating point subject to supplementary validation'). As written, this inconsistency will invite reviewer skepticism of the 100-1,000× expansion claim's empirical grounding.",
    "EVALUATION BREADTH RISK (PERSISTENT, STRUCTURAL): Two primary tasks with sim-only evaluation remains the most probable single cause of CoRL 2026 rejection. The comparison table and depth-vs.-breadth defense are present and well-argued, but MoMaGen (4 tasks, real robot), DexMimicGen (9 tasks, real robot), and HumanoidGen (6 tasks) all show greater task breadth at peer-reviewed venues. The contingent elevation of the articulated-object task to co-primary status (3 tasks if Day 11 validates) is the key risk mitigator — make the elevation condition explicit so reviewers see the 3-task outcome as the base plan, not the 2-task outcome.",
    "COMPATIBILITY FILTERING PASS-RATE RANGE IS DERIVED NOT MEASURED: The paper states 'IRM feasibility eliminates approximately 55-75% of random scene-stance pairings... and pose continuity eliminates a further 10-20%' — these percentage ranges are stated as if empirical but derive from preliminary analysis rather than actual runs on the G1. At submission time with actual data, these should be measured values, not ranges. If the ranges are still projections, label them explicitly as such rather than presenting them as measured filtering rates."
  ],
  "feedback": "The paper has resolved all critical issues from Rounds 1-8. Independent search confirms: PR #3259 is accurately characterized, MoMaGen is correctly placed at ICLR 2026, no April-May 2026 concurrent work directly attacks the coupled SE(3)+IRM+combinatorial composition claim, and all cited papers are verified real. One focused fix is needed for Round 10.\n\n**Fix 1 (required, 2 sentences — blocks CoRL submission credibility)**: Resolve the 'preliminary analysis' vs. 'validated operating point' contradiction. Pick one of two paths: (a) If the τ ablation is actually run before submission: replace 'preliminary analysis estimates' with the measured values and 'validated operating point' remains accurate; OR (b) If the ablation is planned for supplementary: change 'validated operating point' to 'target operating point, with sensitivity ablation across τ ∈ {5th, 10th, 25th percentile} reported in supplementary material.' Path (a) is strongly preferred — a 3-point ablation across τ values requires only three additional policy training runs, which can be parallelized within the existing compute budget, and transforms 'preliminary' into 'measured.'\n\n**Fix 2 (recommended, 1 sentence)**: Reframe the articulated-object task as the BASE PLAN for 3 primary tasks rather than a contingent supplementary upgrade. Change the language from 'if the composition engine validates on Task 1 by Day 11, the supplementary task will be elevated to co-primary status' to 'we target 3 primary tasks; the articulated-object task runs on a parallel track from Day 7 and is co-primary if the composition engine validates on Task 1 by Day 11, and supplementary otherwise.' This signals execution confidence rather than hedging on a 2-task fallback.\n\n**Fix 3 (optional, 1 sentence)**: Replace the filtering rate ranges (55-75% IRM rejection, 10-20% pose-continuity rejection) with language making clear whether these are measured or projected: 'Based on preliminary IK feasibility sampling across the P×Q×R scene configuration space, IRM feasibility is estimated to eliminate approximately 55-75%...' — the qualifier eliminates the false precision of unstated uncertainty.\n\nIf Fix 1 is resolved (τ ablation either run or honestly labeled), the remaining structural weakness (sim-only, 2-3 tasks) is acknowledged and defended at the level CoRL reviewers expect from a depth-focused infrastructure paper. This paper should pass Round 10.",
  "reason": "All Round 8 fixes implemented and independently verified; one blocking terminological inconsistency ('preliminary analysis' vs. 'validated operating point') and the persistent sim-only 2-task evaluation risk remain before this can pass."
}
